[38;5;231m●[39m All three complete. Here are the results:

  [1m1. B-tree Balancing[0m

  Splitting occurs when a node exceeds capacity: the middle key promotes upward, splitting into two nodes.
  Merging happens when a node drops below minimum keys—it merges with a sibling and adopts a parent key.
  Redistribution (moving spare keys from siblings) avoids full merges. Balance is guaranteed because all
  leaf-to-root paths traverse the same number of nodes after every split/merge, maintaining O(log n) operations.

  [1m2. Rate Limiter Test Suite[0m

  [1mCore:[0m allowance within quota, rejection at limit, window reset, boundary (exactly at limit passes, +1 fails).
  [1mConcurrency:[0m atomic counter increments, race conditions at boundary. [1mDistributed:[0m state consistency across
  nodes, partition recovery, clock skew tolerance. [1mEdge cases:[0m burst handling across window boundaries,
  zero-limit rejection, clock rewind, per-key independence. Tests verify correctness, timing isolation,
  atomicity, consensus, and anomaly robustness.

  [1m3. Cache Staleness Risks[0m

  [1mFallout:[0m TTL race (stale served before refresh), invalidation miss (update skipped), distributed lag (replica
  divergence), write-through failure (cache without DB), replica divergence (replicas unsync), database rollback

